# DISCLAIMER
This artifact is not intended for producation use. We recommend using this artifact in a sandboxed environment, such as the provided docker container.
By default, the aritfact can __not__ incur cost by quering a 3rd party LLM provider, however, if you configure the arifact with a LLM provider's API keys, you __will__ incur $ cost when running the artifact.

# Contents
This artifact contains the following:
- instructions for reproducing our results (in this README)
- a docker container with the runtime environment to run our code (vert.tar)
- the artifacts used to create the docker container (Dockerfile, requirements.txt, and .aws/ folder)
- the code used in our experiments (in the vert/ directory)
- the benchmark C, C++, and Go code we translated (in the vert/benchmarks/ directory)
- each benchmark directory contains the following:
	- the input source program, which is a .c/.cpp/.go file named after the benchmark
	- the logged LLM inputs and outputs in the prompt_log/ directory
	- the translation produced by our tool in the file translation/src/translation.rs
	- all other files are intermediate files produced during translation

# Requirements
The only requirements for running our artifact is Docker.
It is also assumed you are using a unix environment.

# Setup
First, load the provided container with the command:

docker load -i vert.tar

Next, start up the docker container in an interactive shell.
You will need to mount the contents of this artifact to the /mnt folder in the container.
Run the following command from the some directory as this README.

sudo docker run -it -v $( pwd ):/mnt vert:latest

In the docker shell, navigate to the vert/ directory:

cd /mnt/vert

Next, test that translation is working by running the below command. The command below will run translation end-to-end, and will replay logged llm responses from the cache.

python3 torust.py --language c --benchmark-dir benchmark/c_transcoder/BASIC_AND_EXTENDED_EUCLIDEAN_ALGORITHMS --replay-cache

# Reproducing Our Results
This artifact can replicate the Claude-2 section of Table 1 without the need for querying a real LLM. In addition, the artifact supports using Anthropic Claude LLMs hosted on Amazon Bedrock out of the box. Adding support for a other LLMs hosted locally or by other 3rd party LLM providers can be done by extending the LLMQuery base class in llm.py. The Claude implementation can be used as a reference.

Reproducing the entire Claude-2 section in Table 1 takes 40+ hours when running each experiment sequentially, so we provide two reproducation scripts: run_small.sh and run_all.sh. The first reproduces the entire Claude-2 results, and second reproduces a subset of our benchmarks. You can run small script as shown below:

bash run_small.sh

